./manual/CHANGESLOG.md:2887:`PakettiBeatsyncSeamlessAutoChop()` automates the live-usage workaround for Renoise's `beat_sync_lines = 512` cap so a sample longer than the cap can still play perfectly in time with the song. The pipeline runs in a single undo step (`Paketti Beatsync Seamless Auto-Chop`): scan the selected sample for the first and last frame above -60 dB peak and trim the leading/trailing silence; normalize the buffer to 0 dBFS via `sample_buffer:normalize()`; read `transport.bpm` and `transport.lpb` and compute `needed_lines = ceil(seconds × bpm × lpb / 60)`, `N = ceil(needed_lines / 512)`, `per_chunk = ceil(needed_lines / N)` so that every chunk fits inside the 512-line cap; duplicate the sample into N equal pieces (`floor(total_frames / N)` frames each) by inserting fresh samples at consecutive instrument indices and copying the corresponding frame range from the source, then deleting the source; set `beat_sync_enabled = true`, `beat_sync_lines = per_chunk`, `autoseek = true`, `autofade = false`, `loop_mode = LOOP_MODE_OFF` on every chunk; keyzone the chunks as a drumkit by setting `sample_mapping.note_range = {note, note}`, `base_note = note`, `velocity_range = {0, 127}` chromatically from C-4 upward; resize the current pattern to `per_chunk × N` lines (capped at 512) and write a note for each chunk at line `(i-1) × per_chunk + 1` of the selected sequencer track using the current instrument. The result: triggering the pattern plays the original sample at its original duration, seamlessly stitched from N beatsynced chunks, locked to the song BPM. If the total chopped length exceeds the 512-line pattern cap, the pattern is capped at 512 and only the chunks that fit are placed; the status bar reports `placed X of N chunks`. Reachable from `Main Menu:Tools:Paketti:Samples:Beatsync Seamless (Auto-Chop Long Sample)`, `Sample Editor:Paketti:Process:Beatsync Seamless (Auto-Chop Long Sample)`, the `Global:Paketti:Beatsync Seamless Auto-Chop` keybinding, and the `Paketti:Beatsync Seamless Auto-Chop` MIDI mapping.
./manual/CHANGESLOG.md:2891:`pakettiBeatSyncHackRenderAndRestore()` adds the production move that the Discord conversation asked for: bake the time-stretched playback of a BeatSyncHacked sample into a new WAV, then clamp the original sample's BeatSyncLines back to 512 so the song becomes XRNS-safe. Pipeline: look up the hacked lines value in `PakettiHackCache`, snapshot pattern length / mute states / selection / edit_mode, insert a temporary sequencer track at `master_idx - 1`, set the current pattern's length to `min(512, hacked_lines)`, place a single C-4 note triggering the hacked instrument on the temp track, add extra sequencer slots referencing the same pattern when the hacked value exceeds 512 (so the render covers the full stretched span), mute every other sequencer track, and call `song:render` from the start of the current sequence slot to the end of the last added slot. On completion the rendered WAV loads as a new sample at the end of `instr.samples`, named `<original> (BSH render)`, with BeatSync disabled. The original sample's `beat_sync_lines` is clamped to 512, `beat_sync_enabled` is set to false, and the cache entry is cleared — the song is now safe to save as XRNS. Teardown deletes the temp track, deletes the extra sequence slots, restores pattern length, mute states, edit_mode, and instrument selection. Reachable from `Main Menu:Tools:Paketti:Xperimental/WIP:BeatSyncHack:Render & Restore Current Sample`, `Sample Editor:Paketti:BeatSyncHack:Render & Restore Current Sample`, `Instrument Box:Paketti:BeatSyncHack:Render & Restore Current Sample`, the "Render Current Sample + Restore BeatSync" button in the BeatSyncHack dialog, and the `Global:Paketti:BeatSyncHack Render & Restore` keybinding.
./manual/CHANGESLOG.md:2899:`PakettiCalculator.lua` adds a lightweight calculator dialog so you don't have to Alt-Tab out of Renoise for the constant arithmetic that tracker work generates: BPM math, ms-per-line, hex effect values, LPB/groove conversions, cents↔ratio, semitones↔ratio. Expressions are full Lua math (sandboxed via `loadstring` + `setfenv`), with extra helpers exposed: `bpm()`, `lpb()`, `tpl()`, `ms_per_line()`, `ms_per_beat()`, `hz_from_bpm()`, `cents_to_ratio(c)`, `ratio_to_cents(r)`, `semi_to_ratio(s)`, `ratio_to_semi(r)`, plus the previous result as `ans`. Every result is shown four ways: decimal (with float precision), rounded integer, full hex (`0xNNN`), and a byte string (`00`-`FF` when in range) — the byte form is what you actually paste into pattern effect amounts. Four one-click "Insert into selected column" buttons send the rounded value to the currently selected effect amount, volume column, panning column, or delay column. A 12-line history pane keeps recent `expression = result` lines visible. Enter/Return triggers `=`, Esc closes. Reachable from `Main Menu:Tools:Paketti:Calculator...`, right-click in Pattern Editor / Sample Editor / Instrument Box, and `Global:Paketti:Calculator Dialog` keybinding. Also registered in the Dialog of Dialogs as "Paketti Calculator".
./manual/CHANGESLOG.md:2903:`PakettiHack.lua` introduces a generalisable hack pipeline for pushing XRNI XML values past their UI-clamped API limits. The first hack ships against `BeatSyncLines`, whose Renoise API range is `1..512` but whose XML loader accepts much larger values (8192, 16384, 32768). The pipeline saves the currently selected instrument to a temp `.xrni`, extracts `Instrument.xml`, patches the Nth `<BeatSyncLines>` block (matching `selected_sample_index`), zips the patched XML back into the same archive (preserving `SampleData/`), and reloads it into the same instrument slot. Instrument name and sample selection are restored after reload. Beat sync is auto-enabled on the target sample so the patched value takes effect. Adds a dialog with one-click preset buttons (`1024`, `2048`, `4096`, `8192`, `16384`, `32768`) and a custom value box up to `65535`, plus standalone menu and keybinding entries per preset. Menu: `Main Menu:Tools:Paketti:Xperimental/WIP:BeatSyncHack:Set BeatSyncLines <N>`, `Main Menu:Tools:Paketti:Xperimental/WIP:BeatSyncHack:Set BeatSyncLines Dialog...`, `Sample Editor:Paketti:BeatSyncHack:Set BeatSyncLines <N>`, `Sample Editor:Paketti:BeatSyncHack:Set BeatSyncLines Dialog...`, `Instrument Box:Paketti:BeatSyncHack:Set BeatSyncLines <N>`, `Instrument Box:Paketti:BeatSyncHack:Set BeatSyncLines Dialog...`. Keybindings: `Global:Paketti:Set BeatSyncLines <N>` (for each preset), `Global:Paketti:Set BeatSyncLines Dialog`. Currently shells out to `unzip`/`zip`, so macOS/Linux only; Windows shows a status message and exits.
./manual/CHANGESLOG.md:2945:Correct semantics, confirmed: for `BOOM TCHIK CLAP TCHIK`, triggering phrase 1 plays `BOOM TCHIK CLAP TCHIK`; phrase 2 plays `TCHIK CLAP TCHIK`; phrase 3 plays `CLAP TCHIK`; phrase 4 plays `TCHIK`. One looping phrase per starting slice, mapped to consecutive notes (C#0, D-0, D#0, …). This is exactly what `pakettiSlicesToPhrasesPerSlice` already does.
./manual/CHANGESLOG.md:2947:Fix: `WipeSliceAndPhraseContinuous(N)` now calls `pakettiSlicesToPhrasesPerSlice(false, true)` with a new `in_place` parameter on that function (mirroring the one added to `pakettiSlicesToPhrase` earlier today). The `in_place` mode wipes existing phrase mappings + phrases on the current instrument and rebuilds them — no instrument duplicate. The dropped looping-on-single-phrase logic is gone.
